The old static `X.modifiers(...)` first-call entry points are **removed** —
`modifiers(...)` is an **instance** operation (interface default for bare `View`
values, covariant override on the builder). Static `X.children(...)` is **kept**
as a value-less first-call convenience (`HStack.children(a, b)` ≡
`HStack.make().children(a, b)`); `make(...)` is the only values static.

**Optional block children (Java).** A nontrivial tree MAY spell the same
`children` operation as a **block collector** instead of varargs:

```
VStack.make(v -> v.spacing(8)).children(content -> {
    content.add(a);
    content.add(HStack.make().children(row -> {
        row.add(b);
        row.add(c);
    }));
});
```

This is pure sugar for `.children(a, HStack.make().children(b, c))` — the
collected views pass through the same `setChildren(List<View>)` path, so both
forms emit **byte-identical opcodes**. The block body gives each structural
element a brace-delimited scope, so IDE fold/indent follows the UI tree. It is an
**optional alternative**: the varargs form stays the canonical spelling. The
three operations are unchanged, and nested block lambdas need distinct parameter
names (Java forbids shadowing).

**Values are a config-builder lambda.**

