      .modifiers(modifier, ...)       // modifiers — varargs
      .children(content -> { ... })   // children  — block, content-bearing views only
```

In the Java realization a view's values entry is the static `make(...)` (the
construction factory; empty `make()` for config-less or value-less starts).
`modifiers(...)` is an **instance** operation available on the builder and on any
`View` value (the interface default), so it (and `children(...)`) chain on the
builder in any order:

```
VStack.make(v -> v.spacing(8)).children(content -> {
    content.add(a);
    content.add(b);
}).modifiers(m)
VStack.make(v -> v.spacing(8)).modifiers(m).children(content -> {
    content.add(a);
    content.add(b);
})
VStack.make().modifiers(m).children(content -> {
    content.add(a);
    content.add(b);
})
```

The old static `X.modifiers(...)` first-call entry points are **removed** —
`modifiers(...)` is an **instance** operation (interface default for bare `View`
values, covariant override on the builder). Static `X.children(content -> …)` is **kept**
as a value-less first-call convenience over the same block
(`HStack.children(content -> { … })` ≡
`HStack.make().children(content -> { … })`); `make(...)` is the only values
static.

**Block children (Java).** The Java realization spells the `children` operation
as a **block collector** — the `ChildScope` passed to the lambda receives each
child in call order:

```
VStack.make(v -> v.spacing(8)).children(content -> {
    content.add(a);
    content.add(HStack.make().children(row -> {
        row.add(b);
        row.add(c);
    }));
});
```

The block body gives each structural element a brace-delimited scope, so IDE
fold/indent follows the UI tree. The collected views pass through
`setChildren(List<View>)` (the `children` operation is the only child form; there
is no varargs spelling in the Java realization). The three operations are
unchanged. Nested block lambdas need distinct parameter names (Java forbids
shadowing).

